{
  "plugin_slug": "hostinger-reach",
  "analysis_summary": "Phase 2 deep analysis of hostinger-reach v1.4.4. Three REST anomalies from IR were investigated: (1) GET /integrations with __return_true returns non-sensitive enumeration data with no secrets; (2) POST /contact with __return_true is protected by an in-handler wp_rest nonce check which, combined with the requirement that the site be connected, reduces exploitability; (3) POST /optinmonster with __return_true is similarly protected by an in-handler wp_rest nonce check. The wp_rest nonce IS generated for unauthenticated visitors on WordPress (since WP 5.3), making the nonce-only check on public REST routes a meaningful bypass opportunity but requiring the attacker to first obtain a valid nonce from a page where it is embedded. For /optinmonster, the nonce is localised to every page load when the integration is active, making it effectively public. This constitutes a finding. AddFormNotice AJAX handler is missing a capability check — any authenticated user can trigger it (nonce guards are present but no role check). Semgrep-flagged XSS signals in SatisfactionSurvey and Functions.php are all FP: the code paths use boolean/server-env values that are not reflected in HTML output. WooCommerce SQL patterns are FP due to hardcoded table names. Unresolved hooks resolved to hardcoded values or admin-only contexts.",
  "overall_risk": "low",
  "total_findings_analyzed": 9,
  "true_positives": 1,
  "false_positives": 7,
  "needs_review": 1,
  "findings": [
    {
      "finding_id": "HR-001",
      "verdict": "TP",
      "vuln_type": "Broken Access Control — Unauthenticated REST Endpoint (subscriber contact injection via obtainable nonce)",
      "cwe_id": "CWE-862",
      "severity": "medium",
      "auth_required": false,
      "confidence": 0.72,
      "entry_point_id": "ep_rest_hostinger_reach_v1__optinmonster",
      "source_function": "submit_optinmonster",
      "sink_function": "do_action('hostinger_reach_submit', ...)",
      "source_file_path": "src/Integrations/OptInMonster/OptInMonsterIntegration.php",
      "source_line_number": 62,
      "sink_file_path": "src/Integrations/OptInMonster/OptInMonsterIntegration.php",
      "sink_line_number": 78,
      "file_path": "src/Integrations/OptInMonster/OptInMonsterIntegration.php",
      "line_number": 78,
      "reasoning": {
        "hypothesis": "An unauthenticated attacker can inject arbitrary email addresses into the Hostinger Reach contact list by POSTing to /wp-json/hostinger-reach/v1/optinmonster because (1) permission_callback is __return_true, and (2) the in-handler nonce check uses the wp_rest nonce which WordPress generates for ALL visitors (including logged-out users) since WP 5.3, and (3) this nonce is embedded in every frontend page load when the OptInMonster integration is active via wp_localize_script.",
        "trace": "Attacker visits any page on the target site where OptInMonster is active → JavaScript bundle contains wp_localize_script data including 'nonce' (wp_create_nonce('wp_rest')) → Attacker reads nonce from page source or XHR response → POST /wp-json/hostinger-reach/v1/optinmonster with X-WP-Nonce header and crafted JSON body containing 'fields' array with attacker-chosen email → OptInMonsterIntegration::submit_optinmonster() (line 62) calls is_authorized() (line 201) which calls wp_verify_nonce($nonce, 'wp_rest') (line 203) — this succeeds because wp_rest nonces are valid for non-logged-in users → find_email() extracts the email from $data['fields'] (line 73) → do_action('hostinger_reach_submit') fires with attacker-controlled email (line 78) → email is sent to Hostinger Reach API as a subscriber",
        "defenses": [
          "permission_callback = __return_true: no restriction at WP REST layer",
          "is_authorized() at line 63: checks wp_verify_nonce($nonce, 'wp_rest') — wp_rest nonces are issued to guest users in WP 5.3+, so this is not an effective barrier against unauthenticated callers who can obtain the nonce from any page",
          "find_email() validates email format via sanitize_email + FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL at line 253-254 — this constrains the value to valid email format but does not restrict who can call the endpoint"
        ],
        "refutation": [
          "REFUTATION ATTEMPT: Does wp_rest nonce restrict to logged-in users only? RESULT: Not refuted. Since WordPress 5.3, wp_create_nonce('wp_rest') generates valid nonces for non-logged-in users (nonce tied to user_id=0). wp_verify_nonce succeeds for any request that presents the nonce obtained from a page view within the 12-24 hour nonce lifetime.",
          "REFUTATION ATTEMPT: Is the nonce exposed only to authenticated users? RESULT: Not refuted. The nonce is embedded via wp_localize_script in enqueue_assets() (line 228-249 of OptInMonsterIntegration.php), which fires on the frontend when the integration is active — i.e., on every public page load.",
          "REFUTATION ATTEMPT: Does the endpoint have meaningful impact? RESULT: Not refuted. The endpoint triggers do_action('hostinger_reach_submit') which calls the Hostinger Reach API to add the supplied email as a subscriber. This allows an unauthenticated actor to inject arbitrary valid email addresses into the site owner's contact list, causing reputational harm and potentially violating GDPR/CAN-SPAM (adding contacts without consent)."
        ],
        "conclusion": "Confirmed TP. The combination of __return_true permission_callback and reliance on a guest-accessible wp_rest nonce for authentication means any user who can fetch a page where OptInMonster is active can submit arbitrary email addresses to the contact list. The fix is to either (a) restrict the permission_callback to is_user_logged_in() or current_user_can('read'), or (b) use a site-specific nonce action that cannot be obtained from the frontend."
      },
      "poc_description": "1. With OptInMonster integration active, visit any page on the target site. 2. In browser devtools or page source, locate the hostinger_reach_optinmonster_data JS object and extract the 'nonce' value. 3. Send: POST /wp-json/hostinger-reach/v1/optinmonster with headers X-WP-Nonce: <extracted_nonce>, Content-Type: application/json and body: {\"formId\":\"\",\"fields\":[\"victim@example.com\"],\"tags\":{\"campaign_name\":\"injected\",\"form_name\":\"test\"}}. 4. Observe 200 response and that the email is added to the Hostinger Reach contact list.",
      "cvss_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N",
      "remediation": "Change permission_callback from __return_true to a callback that verifies the user is logged in (is_user_logged_in()), or use a nonce action that cannot be obtained by unauthenticated visitors. Consider rate-limiting the endpoint.",
      "patchstack_eligible": true
    },
