      "reasoning": {
        "hypothesis": "An authenticated subscriber-level user can dismiss the 'Add Form' admin notice or trigger the 'success' action by sending a valid nonce-protected AJAX request to wp_ajax_hostinger_reach_add_form_notice_action. Unlike ConnectionNotice (which checks current_user_can('manage_options')), AddFormNotice does not perform a capability check.",
        "trace": "AddFormNotice::init() registers wp_ajax_hostinger_reach_add_form_notice_action (line 28) → handle_ajax_action() is called (line 43) → check_ajax_referer(self::NOTICE_ACTION, 'nonce') validates nonce (line 44) → no capability check follows → $choice from $_POST['choice'] is processed → 'dismiss' case sets transient at NOTICE_DISMISS_TRANSIENT, 'success' case returns a redirect URL",
        "defenses": [
          "check_ajax_referer() at line 44: requires a valid nonce for the specific action — limits exposure to users who have a valid nonce",
          "The nonce is only generated and localised in enqueue_notice_assets() which fires inside should_render() → but should_render() does not check current_user_can() — it only checks screen->id (dashboard, pages, posts, edit-page, edit-post) and connection status"
        ],
        "refutation": [
          "REFUTATION ATTEMPT: Is the nonce only delivered to admin users? RESULT: Partially refuted. The nonce is enqueued via admin_enqueue_scripts, which only fires in the admin area. WordPress admin area normally requires at least author-level access, not subscriber. Subscriber-level users cannot access admin screens that match the screen ID whitelist (dashboard requires capability 'read', but admin pages like pages/posts/edit-page/edit-post require higher capabilities). Therefore, a subscriber typically cannot obtain the nonce.",
          "REFUTATION ATTEMPT: Can a subscriber reach an admin screen to obtain the nonce? RESULT: Depends on site configuration. In default WordPress, subscribers cannot access the admin dashboard beyond their profile page. The admin_notices hook only runs on admin pages that the subscriber can access — which by default is only their profile page (not in the whitelist). Therefore exploitation requires non-default configuration or another vulnerability to access the nonce.",
          "REFUTATION ATTEMPT: What is the actual impact if exploited? RESULT: The impact is limited — 'dismiss' sets a transient that hides the notice for one week; 'success' returns a hardcoded redirect URL string. Neither action affects data integrity, credentials, or security settings. This is a design inconsistency (ConnectionNotice has the capability check, AddFormNotice does not) but exploitation in a default WordPress configuration is not straightforward."
        ],
        "conclusion": "REVIEW. There is a missing capability check compared to the sibling ConnectionNotice handler. In default WordPress configurations, a subscriber cannot obtain the nonce, limiting practical exploitability. However, in sites with custom roles or other misconfigurations, a lower-privileged user could trigger the dismiss/success actions. The impact is low (notice management only). Marking as REVIEW rather than TP because the exploitation path requires non-default conditions.",
        "why_uncertain": "Exploitation requires a subscriber to be able to obtain the nonce, which depends on whether the subscriber can access any admin screen matched by the screen ID whitelist. This is configuration-dependent.",
        "resolution_path": "Verify whether subscriber-level or author-level users on a default WordPress installation can access any admin screen that matches screen IDs 'dashboard', 'pages', 'posts', 'edit-page', or 'edit-post' and thus receive the enqueued nonce."
